  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Solve for $y$ where ${81}^{y}={27}^{y+5}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $81={3}^{4}$ and $27={3}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{4})}^{y}={({3}^{3})}^{y+5}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4y}={3}^{3(y+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y & =3(y+5) \\\\ 4y & =3y+15 \\\\ y & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$",
